  },
  {
    "insert": "\n\nThis exposé proposes research into holographic AI assistants as an alternative to digital assistants on flat screens\nor augmented/virtual reality, specifically in the context of education and healthcare. It argues for this novel\ntechnology with an enhanced user experience; a study with participants from the sectors mentioned prior shall be\nconducted to verify that claim.\n\nStarting off with the strengths: In the approach, the text profiles its research against the state of the art, noting \nsome deficits like \"lack of contextual awareness\" in cited papers. The description of the theoretical framework after\ncontains a correct use of a cited paper, summarising it and justifying the use of some methods in the project.\n\nMoving on to the weaknesses: in general, the exposé suffers from irritating lines of thought and an unclear research\nproposal. The review you are reading here explains the most significant ones, others are annotated in CARE.\nThe motivation should include an engaging hook, illustrating a problem to solve or a vision the research can make\nprogress to. Using such rhetorical question and appeal to the reader is not as effective as it may seem at first.\nBecause you are describing an unfounded future vision, it does not make for a convincing statement, quite the opposite;\nwithout any foundation, this part exudes little credibility in your ideas. Missing citations reinforce the problem.\nA total rewrite is necessary with more engaging story telling and statements supported by citing papers or clear\narguments.\nThe aspect of missing citations is something that plagues the entire motivation. Making a factual statement in a \nscientific paper must either be cited or be clearly argued for in the text. The entire section does neither, which\nmakes anything written down sound hollow and non-credible. For example: claiming to have found a research gap upon\nstatements without citations, is not sound. Again, proper citations and connecting arguments and thoughts together\nwould give your exposé some punch.\nThis leads to the research questions (RQs). Those, and thus the entire approach require a clear focus, especially in\nthe context of a thesis and its limited time frame of 24 weeks. This includes a feasible and precise goal and defining\nmetrics that make evaluation possible. Your \"key questions\" are pointing in many different research directions and are\nthemselves in some parts imprecise; efficiency and usability as metrics are great and all if the paragraph prior\nelaborates on them; this is missing. Answering all of them sufficiently is unrealistic as each one of them needs to\ncollect specific kinds of data with empirical methods (RQ1 & 2), technical analysis (RQ3), or ethical assessment (RQ4).\nIn short: the approach has to cover multiple and different kinds of research methods. Without regarding your approach\nand schedule in the exposé, this is unlikely to happen in 24 weeks. The sensible solution is the cutting of some RQs\nand a refinement of those left after. The last aspect is necessary with respect to the definition of a clear goal,\nwhich needs to be stated more precisely, too.\n\nOn the topic of the approach: it should contain convincing descriptions of the state of the art and a positioning of\nyour research there and a sensible theoretical framework and methodology supporting your research questions. Besides\nthe one strength mentioned prior, this is overall insufficient for a future research project, primarily due to two\npoints elaborated in the following paragraphs.\nYour theoretical framework must lay the groundwork for your methodology by enabling a path to significant results, be\nit ways of conducting a study or useful metrics. At one point, the theoretical framework mentions theory which is not\ncited nor argued for. This puts a dent into its credibility. Furthermore, you did not connect the theories together and\nwith the methodology. It just declares those two theories as the framework without putting it into the research context\nlike describing their significance or critiquing aspects. Cite the second theory or cut it out, and explain why the\ntheories mentioned matter in your research, like extracting metrics. This may include a reason for the citation of two\npapers specifically (thus implicitly comparing them).\nAt last, a critique about the methodology. It should describe a realistic way (with respect to 24 weeks) and sensible\nmethods to gain results to answer the RQs. Especially with multiple different RQs, the methodology has to be well\nthought out and contain a collection of methods. Multiple methods are mentioned; neither is their purpose in the\nresearch argued for nor are they planned out well enough to formulate expectations, e.g. the part \"[...] gathering \nin-depth feedback on the interaction [...]\" does not clear the way to answer any RQs. The missing metrics do not help\nthat case, even if they are listed in an unrelated sentence after that should have been part of the theoretical \nframework. In addition, the unrealistic amount of participants, unpleasant transition into describing the other part of\nyour research and a statement with missing citations (\"Preliminary studies [...]\") further reinforce the impression,\nthat not much care went into the planning process. A complete revision of this section is necessary in accordance to \nnew RQs if done so as suggested. Put a focus onto bridging the gap between method and RQ and scale down.\n\nSome miscellaneous notes follow, including some about the schedule and formalities. Due to the weaknesses in the text\nitself, the schedule itself must be revised anyway; it should have been a chronology for your potential future work to\nmake the process as smooth as possible. Some things to fix are: the listing of tasks which should have been handled in\nthis exposé, like identifying research gaps or formulating RQs(!), the vagueness of task description, a result of the\nunclear research proposal, and the submission task being about a exposé, not a thesis.\nAnother aspect are wrong citations and, subjectively speaking, awkward language; sentences do not flow, sections are\ntagged in the approach and the line of thought sometimes becomes fuzzy. Some of those issues are marked in CARE.\nThe solutions for those problems should be clear.\n\nAll in all, the exposé has many glaring issues throughout, with few redeeming qualities. The major problem is the too\nbroad research scope, creating a very unclear approach, which itself contains pit falls. This cascades into many\nminor problems; combining that with the formalities and the result is a exposé which needs a lot of work.\n\n"
  }
]